[{:seon.task/name "a-program-store-holds-only-the-attributes-its-key-names"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-14 (data and tests, BLOCKERS): the shared program store's key (seon.test-store/program-schema-digest) digests only the program's attributes, but seon.db/open! installs every attribute of the binary's schema, so a branch that changes another attribute's type (jolt/data's :seon.proof/* change) shares the key and is refused :seon.db/types-differ, and test-runner/program-db moved the store aside under other sessions' readers (removed while held, before tests' e16deb67c0; now a cold import each time, a cost). Change: seon.db/open! takes a schema subset, the attributes the store's key names; the program store installs only those, so types-differ cannot fire within a key and program-db's remove branch is deleted. Keep: unrelated attributes keep the store (test_store_test.clj:137)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-program-store-holds-only-the-attributes-its-key-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-history-window-per-branch-collects-old-commits"
  :seon.task/plan "Designed, held by the owner's ruling (2026-11-13: store history is kept whole for now). The store is 1.27 GB for 361k live datoms, about 95% old commits' index nodes (325k stored keys, 4,139 commits, measured 11-13). Datahike's own GC takes a keep set and a dry run (datahike.api/gc-storage :datahike.gc/keep-commits, :datahike.gc/sweep-opts {:konserve.gc/dry-run? true}); measured dry runs keeping each branch's last n commits: n=1 frees 56% of nodes, 10 frees 54%, 100 frees 39%, 1000 frees 1.5%. The change when released: seon.db/retain! keeps the last :seon.config.store/keep-commits commits of every branch (today main's chain only, which never reached 1000), plus each head, merge base and named commit, through the same gc-storage call; merged agent branches past a config age are deleted so collect! reclaims them. Every holder of a commit id read later (a page's basis, a turn's request, a proof's basis) names it, as agents' commits are named. Measured 2026-11-14 (data, at root's REPL): retain! runs at boot today (owner's 11-06 setup, kept by root 11-14) and has swept 4 times (gen 1005, 2023, 3309, 5301: 482k keys; the last took 37 s of boot). Its keep set takes d/merge-base for each branch: 63 branches cost 1.1B ticks, 4.8 GB and 5.2 s, and an ExceptionInfo from merge-base other than the several-bases refusal is kept as \"keep nothing\" for that branch (a branch dropped mid-scan answered \"Branch does not exist\"). The design keeps bases by what is recorded (each branch's base as its agent's facts name it), not a merge-base walk a boot, and an error from it is the sweep's refusal, never an empty keep."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-history-window-keeps-each-branchs-last-commits}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-shared-interfaces-are-in-the-wiki"
  :seon.task/plan "The interfaces agreed 2026-11-13 across runtime, web, tests, deps and data, and landed at 153ca44a53, are said in the wiki, each in its mechanism's section: the bound (:seon.bound/bound, ticks bytes rss ms items; :seon.bound/reached with bound, spent and at; a function's bound in its contract's props, a test's from its estimate by one rule) in docs/flow.md; the effect answer (:seon.fx/answer, :seon.fx/read, :seon.fx/reads, a file read's digest its git blob id; the shell is my.shell/sh) in docs/flow.md#effects-one-owner-and-its-workers-per-kind; a run's reads (:seon.reads/set, outside reads as :seon.fx/read, :seon.reads/unreusable) and the reader index (seon.db/reads-add, reads-drop, readers) in docs/storage.md#what-a-read-scanned (done); a test's result (:seon.proof/proof-fact, keyed by its test and the content it read, status written once) in docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache. Today they are only in data's notes (plans/data-agreed-11-13.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/every-section-of-the-wiki-has-a-lead}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-checked-write-costs-what-it-checks"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by tests' slow-test work, 2026-11-13: `seon.db/checked` spends about 7 M ticks checking a two-file program's import, where docs/cost.md#what-things-cost-here puts a ~10-datom transaction at ~50 k ticks, so the import's few hundred datoms at under 1 M (55 M across the 8 small imports of seon.proofs-test/a-plan-reads-the-kept-program-db-of-its-tree); and `seon.db/seed!` about 18 M a fresh store (59 M with seon.db/install-missing! in a fixture that was not warmed). First split each by its parts, measured in ticks (seon.cost/measure): the per-datom value check (seon.db/invalid-datom), the per-entity schema check (seon.db/invalid-entity), the speculation, the index the check reads (seon.db/index-of), the Datahike transact; then fix the part over its share at its cause, with the estimate stated as arithmetic before the change. Done: a checked write of n datoms within 2x of n times the per-datom unit, held by a cost proof at n = 10, 100, 300."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-checked-write-costs-what-it-checks}}
 {:seon.task/name "two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Since task each-id-is-issued-once (worktree session global-ids, 2026-10-23) entity ids and transaction ids come from one page every process shares (seon.db/ids!), reseeded from the heads at open: of what follows, only the roster append remains. Several processes writing different branches of one store is safe and is the design: each commits its own branch (content-addressed immutable node keys plus its one head key, each put one LMDB write transaction; konserve.jolt.lmdb:86). Changing the roster is not: `d/branch!` (``datahike.versioning/branch!``) appends the :branches log with ``konserve.core/append`` (reference-code/konserve src/konserve/core.cljc:570), which reads the log head and then makes three writes (the element, the old last element's :next, the head) under go-locked, a lock of this process only (konserve.jolt.lmdb's blob lock is a no-op token, lmdb.jolt:28). Two processes branching at once can each link after the same last element, and one branch drops out of `d/branches` (its head key stays, so `seon.db/branch?` still finds it); a process SIGKILLed between the writes leaves the same tear. Production has one root per store, the only process that branches (the writer's :spawn and :clone-branch); several roots on one store (parallel check steps as separate roots, a standing bin/system beside a T5 run on <SEON_RUNS>/store) meet both. Correct: either the roster append is one LMDB write transaction (a konserve.jolt.lmdb -update-in over the three keys in one txn, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our konserve), or one root per store at a time is a failure point with its edge (`seon.db/open!` refusing a store another live root holds, e.g. by an flock on <store>/root.lock). Parallel checks share one root, each step on its own branch (production's shape), with the crash steps (durable, window, resume, orphan) run with nothing else on the store. The named proof exists and is green on 2026-11-06 (`bin/test seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store`, SHARD line read: two processes make 50 branches each, every branch is listed), so it does not fail on the tear described here. docs/storage.md#collection says production runs one root per store and gives `:seon.config.store/sole-writer?`. What refuses a second system started on the same store (read 2026-11-11, Tests, notes store-resume-facts-2026-11-11.md): nothing. bin/system's running check reads its own runs directory's root.pids (bin/system:63-66), no pid or port is kept beside the store, and LMDB's lock lets several processes in; so a second root on a store is the case this task's roster race takes, by mistake as easily as on purpose. The proof should include it: a second root opened on a store a root holds is refused, typed, naming the holder."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-branch-one-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-collection-runs-beside-live-agents"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: root collects the store only at quiescence with no agent live: `seon.reconcile/quiet` asks once per quiet spell, root's api refuses :collect while an agent is live, and the writer runs `seon.db/collect!` (docs/storage.md#collection). A commit can reuse a value out of line that a concurrent sweep would take, so on a busy root garbage from deleted branches waits for a quiet spell. Correct (the design in docs/storage.md#collection): the mark runs outside LMDB's writer lock; a short delta mark and the sweep run inside one write transaction; a commit that reuses a value re-asserts it in its own transaction. Deletes the api's no-agent-live reason of :collect (seon.system, the :write clause refusing :collect while `(:agents st)` is non-empty). The named proof is not written; docs/storage.md#collection says collecting beside live agents is not built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collection-beside-a-committing-agent-keeps-every-value-it-reuses}}
 {:seon.task/name "values-compress-with-a-trained-dictionary"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every stored value is compressed by the codec `:seon.config.store/compressor` names (zstd level 3, one frame per value, `konserve.jolt.zstd`), each value's header naming its codec (docs/storage.md#compression). Measured there: a dictionary trained on our own values compresses small ones 2-5x better, and faster; it is unused because it needs a corpus that represents the running system. Correct: a dictionary trained (zstd's ZDICT_trainFromBuffer) on a kept store's values, stored in the store under its digest; a value's header names the dictionary, so a store holding values written with and without one reads both; retraining adds a dictionary and never rewrites old values. A konserve fork change on a jolt/<topic> branch, root's go first. Measure bytes per per-turn commit first. Neither named proof is written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-value-written-with-a-dictionary-reads-back seon.db-test/a-dictionary-shrinks-what-a-commit-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "opening-a-store-costs-what-it-opens"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists on main: `seon.db/ids!` trusts an ids page whose stamp (the OS boot identity, the ids file's and the data file's device and inode) is current and reads no branch head; it reads the heads (``datahike.writing/set-ids!``, O(branches)) only for a new, copied or remade page or data file, or after a reboot, then stamps (docs/storage.md#ids). A stamped open is flat in branches: 0.37 M ticks, 1.86 MB and 10 konserve reads at 1, 10, 100 and 1,000 branches (measured 2026-11-10; proofs green on main since batch 22: `seon.db-test/an-open-reads-no-head-but-its-own`, `seon.db-test/a-page-from-another-boot-is-moved-past-the-heads`, `seon.db-test/a-copied-or-new-page-is-moved-past-the-heads`, `seon.db-test/a-page-beside-a-replaced-data-file-is-moved-past-the-heads`). ``test-runner/program-db`` asks for its tree's branch alone (`seon.db/branch?`) and lists the branches only for a tree to import; what ``d/branches`` costs is the roster's (task ``datahike.versioning/the-branch-roster-is-compacted``). What remains: `seon.db/install-missing!` reads every attribute of the schema against the store's `(:schema db)` on each open, though nothing is missing (1.35 MB a call, measured 2026-10-27, not measured since); one compare of the store's schema digest against the program's would answer the common case. And a fresh store's schema costs ~22 M ticks to install (measured 2026-10-31: `seon.db/seed!` 259 M of 317 M ticks for 12 fresh stores, nearly all in install-missing!), which every test with its own store and every check seed pays; a fresh store could cost what a copy of a seeded one does. First step: measure `seon.db/open!` of a stamped store again, split by function, so the next cut is named by number. Proof when built: an open of a stamped store whose schema matches costs a stated number, flat in attributes and branches."
  :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/opening-a-store-costs-what-it-opens}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-roots-heap-costs-what-it-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Every agent inherits the fork server's heap, so an agent's start heap is root's live heap: 0.97 GB on a fresh store and 1.32 GB on the standing one (355k datoms against 178k; seon.agent at agent.clj caps it at that plus :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes). The 0.36 GB grows with every recorded test run (106k :seon.proof* datoms) and every write (48k seon.tx and db datoms), with no bound. Measured 2026-10-28 on an APFS clone of runs/store, at main d96168afb1:
(1) The node cache is not the cause. :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes bounds it, but by count: datahike.index.node-cache weighs every node at 4,096 bytes, so the bound is 32,768 nodes. A just-booted standing root fills it (25k misses in 20 s; 12k on a fresh store, so boot reads in proportion to the store, not to what it needs). Evicting all 32,768 nodes and collecting the maximum generation freed 50 MB (1.419 -> 1.367 GB live).
(2) Realized values held outside the cache are the cause. Chez object-counts after a full collection, standing against fresh: vectors 101 MB (1.80 M) against 29 MB (352k), strings 183 MB against 104 MB, pairs 82 MB against 63 MB, bytevectors 27 MB against 18 MB: +180 MB of dynamic objects that a value root holds with the cache empty. Live heap after collection: 1.367 GB against 1.034 GB. The shape (1.45 M more vectors, 170k more strings) fits realized datoms or hiccup.
(3) The index trees hold nothing realized: (:eavt db)'s _root is nil (restored by address on each read), so persistent-sorted-set's Branch _children memo (node-child) retains no tree on the live db.
Not yet known: which of root's values holds (2). compute-size over 'static reaches the whole program (952 MB for every component), so it cannot attribute. The next step is a census by boot phase (object-counts after each seon.system/boot! step) or by holder (the render cache, :results, the task index, derived problem classes), on a clone of runs/store, never on the standing root.
The fix is at the producer that pulls the whole store: it reads what its view needs, through the bounded node cache, and the cache weighs a node by its real size. Owner rule: never pull the whole db. Checked 2026-11-06: (1)'s weight and bound are no longer so: datahike.index.node-cache weighs a node by `node-bytes` (its real size, node_cache.clj:99) and `:seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes` is 134,217,728 (128 MiB); the named proof (seon.system-cost-test) is not written and test/seon has no such namespace."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-cost-test/a-roots-live-heap-is-flat-in-its-recorded-runs}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-idle-root-reads-nothing-from-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-29 (scratch root of main at 200,744 datoms, node cache under a 1 GiB bound so nothing evicts; docs/machine.md#roots-heap): after its boot reads (7,400 nodes, 136 MB weighed) an idle root's cache keeps growing, 1.6 nodes and about 200 hits a second, 0.5 MB every 20 s, with no task, message or commit arriving. Something reads main's indexes on a poll or a render with no input changed (docs/README.md: if nothing has changed, we do nothing). Find the reader (a sampled stack of the root's fibers, or the cache's misses by address and the datoms they hold), fix it at the producer so an idle root's hits and misses stay at zero. Proof: a cost proof that the cache's hits and misses over an idle interval after boot are zero."
  :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-idle-root-reads-nothing-from-the-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-node-cache-is-sized-from-what-an-agent-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "docs/machine.md#roots-heap and :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes: the bound is one key read by the root, the fork server and every agent, and only root's read set is measured (136 MB weighed for main at 200,744 datoms, about 0.7 KB weighed a datom, against a 128 MiB bound). An agent inherits the fork server's cache (preload! is deleted: what the fork server read before the fork) and adds its own reads. RUNTIME MEASURED, NOT YET REPRODUCED BY DEPS (2026-11-16): the fork server's cache holds 256 MB of real Chez bytes against the 128 MiB bound: compute-size of (:store db) at a fresh agent is 256 MB, the same as db and the reconciler index, which hold it (result/d1623d16); tests2's census of that process shows 140 MB of strings (608k) and 117 MB of pairs. So datahike.index.node-cache/node-bytes undercounts ~2x, or something besides the cache holds those nodes: settle which at the REPL first (compute-size of the cache alone, then node-bytes against compute-size per node). GOAL: the bound counts the bytes the cache really holds. TESTS2 MEASURED (2026-11-16, agent g3305b3d4c753089): the cache is full, 10,038 nodes weighed at 134,208,044 of 134,217,728 bytes, 20,506 evictions, holding what loading main read (form source, code, plans, pages; docstrings ~8 MB), not what agents read; and a hit sets its entry's recency flag, a write into an inherited page that copies it into the fork. DATA MEASURED (2026-11-16, result/ba5cb3ba): the cache weighs itself at its 134 MB bound, but emptying it after a full collection freed 213 MB: node-bytes under-weighs what an entry retains, ~1.6x. CAUSE MEASURED BY DEPS (agent ga265cad25a9dab8, result/c0d95900, c39fc2e0): node-bytes is accurate, within 4-7% of Chez compute-size over the cached leaves sized jointly (162.5 MB real, 156.6 estimated); but the cache books a leaf once, at put!, and a leaf grows after it when its values are decoded on read (Datahike's a-leaf-decodes-only-the-values-read): leaves booked 112.7 MB, 155.6 MB now, 966 of 11,172 entries grown; branches booked 21.5, real 18.6, holding no children. FIX: where a lazily decoded value is attached to a cached leaf, the cache's total grows by its bytes and shrinks to the bound, so the bound counts what the cache holds, at O(1) a decode. DONE ON jolt/deps (datahike 26f4b75b, persistent-sorted-set cca1f22): a large value read is held by the cache under its own weighed entry, no LazyValue memo, no read-key write-back. Same workload (6,637 sources read) on a fresh agent: booked 134.2 MB = 134.2 MB held (1.00x), against 134.2 booked / 186.9 held (1.39x) on main's pins; 39.3M ticks against 42.0M. Three designs, decided by the private gauge (vmmap SM=PRV dirty, by pid), never phys_footprint: (A) the system process forks with its node cache empty, each agent's cache filling with what it reads; (B) the cache is inherited and a hit writes nothing into an inherited entry; (C) (root) the cache sized from what forks read, with node-bytes made true. Against (A), unreproduced: deps' predecessor measured a fresh agent's private memory doubling with the bound at 16 MiB. Measure a fresh agent's private bytes at spawn and after one eval and one search under (A), (B) and today. DECISION RULE (root, 2026-11-16): A is the default, since boot builds nothing that outlives it; within ~10% the simpler wins; B only if it wins clearly. After the partial load. Measure what agents add on real tasks: an agent's node-cache-stats (hits, misses, weighed bytes) at seon_done over e2e tasks and one real task, and whether main's whole index belongs in the fork server's cache at all. Then size agents' bound from that, with a profile or a role key only if the measurement says they differ."
  :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-agents-node-cache-is-sized-from-what-an-agent-reads}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-held-db-is-a-reference"
  :seon.task/plan "A db value kept past its moment pins its whole version of the index (a kept db value is a reference: docs/storage.md#a-kept-db-value-is-a-reference; measured 2026-10-28: the render, vectors and reconciler procs each held ~160 MB, root's *e 1.08 GB), and today each holder works around it by hand (\"keep the commit, let the db go\" in seon.writer and seon.db/at-commit; seon.repl/walked-db and the :walk-db no longer exist in src on 2026-11-06). The owner, 2026-10-29: \"maybe we should make the db just be metadata and if you deref it you get the db ... anyone holding or getting a db is just the three metadata keys we need to retrieve it so they can go anywhere\". What exists since: `seon.db/point` (a db's [commit-id max-tx]) and `seon.db/at-commit` (the db again from a commit), which `seon.agent`'s reach graph already keys by (docs/machine.md#roots-heap); not yet one {:branch :commit :t} map with one deref. Make the reference one value: {:branch :commit :t}, plain data (it prints, crosses the wire to an agent, sits in a fact or an error), and one function in seon.db that derefs it through a cache keyed by commit (derived data: evictable, a miss is seon.db/at-commit). Measured 2026-10-29 on the bench (a 40-commit branch): making an old commit's db 0.28 ms and 412 KB, its first query 0.97 ms, the next 0.10 ms. Scope, the owner's choice of the narrow form: references wherever a db is held (process state, caches, errors, proc state, messages to agents, the walk's :walk-db), the ~950 functions and calls that take a db value within one call (426 lines in 32 files of src and src/my name :seon.host/db on 2026-11-06) left as they are. Root is an agent on main: its seon.agent/db derefs {:branch :main} (today (seon.agent/db) in a root eval throws \"no branch\"), which replaces seon.repl's agent-only condition on reading an eval's citations. Merges first as its own small task (the reference and its deref, with a cost proof); then the held sites move over in order of measured memory."
  :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-held-db-is-a-reference}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-of-a-few-datoms-writes-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-01 in a test root's agent, 30 commits of one eval record each (seon.agent/commit!, docs/machine.md#an-agents-first-write): a commit of a few datoms allocates 2.3-2.6 MB, flat over the 30 (db->stored 0.36-0.6 MB, datahike.writing/create-commit-id 0.12 MB, konserve.core/multi-assoc 1.7-1.9 MB). Each writes 21-25 values: the leaf and two to three branch levels of each index whose path it changed (current and history, :keep-history? true, v1's ruling) and three records (the commit graph entry, the commit, the branch head); the nodes' contents weigh ~200 KB (datahike.index.node-cache/node-bytes), so the write path allocates ~9x what it writes. Split on one commit's 18 nodes (1.56 MB, ~87 KB a node): konserve.impl.defaults/prepare-multi-assoc's own work 875 KB (each value serialized through a ByteArrayOutputStream, whose buffer doubles, then toByteArray, a copy; the node's write handler), konserve.jolt.zstd/compress 244 KB and konserve.jolt.fasl/encode 231 KB in 36 calls each (half of them a per-value metadata map, encoded and compressed like a node), and the LMDB write 139 KB (konserve.jolt.lmdb's concat-bytes copying header, meta and value into one array, one write transaction per key). Every eval of every agent pays it. The change, in our forks (konserve, then datahike if a part is there), each step measured before and after on the same commit: (1) split prepare-multi-assoc's 875 KB (the buffer and copy against the node handler's data) with seon.profile/counted wraps, as the measurement above did; (2) a value is serialized once into a buffer of its size (fasl's encode-bv, no ByteArrayOutputStream growth and copy); (3) a batch's uniform metadata ({:immutable? true}) encoded once and never compressed (a small map that zstd grows); (4) the LMDB backing writes a batch in one write transaction, header, meta and value put into the reserved value (MDB_RESERVE) without concat-bytes. Estimate: 2.4 MB to under 1.2 MB a commit; a measured number, not this one, goes in the commit. Done 2026-11-01 (konserve 5d89b976, jolt/serialize-once): (2) and (4)'s copy, a commit 2.3-2.6 MB -> 1.74-1.90 MB (seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once); (1) measured: of a batch's 1.08 MB after it, fasl's encoder (konserve.jolt.fasl, jfasl.ss kjc-encode) allocates ~20 KB a node for its ~2.6 KB of bytes (its buffer doubles from 256; every keyword's namespace and name through string->utf8 at every datom: a cache of a keyword's encoded name is the next step), zstd ~13 KB a value, freeze ~9 KB; (3) left: a value's metadata is compressed by the header's one compressor, so not compressing it changes the stored format and its reader. Not changed: what is written (the paths, the history indexes). The other lever, committing per turn rather than per eval, is the run-ahead writer's rule (seon.agent/commit!: when the agent is idle or at a turn's end) and the owner's to change. Proofs: a cost proof of one commit's bytes in seon.db (bytes and contracted calls exact at a fixed input, a commit of 1, 10 and 100 datoms: what the paths cost, never 9x), seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run with its :fixed lowered by what begin-run!'s first commit no longer allocates, and the forks' own gates (a pin bump runs every Jolt and fork gate: docs/merging.md#conflicts-and-pins). Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.db/a-one-eval-commit-costs-what-it-writes (Measured 2026-10-24 on jolt/agent-conversation (an agent commits after each eval): inside an agent, one model eval of (+ 20 21) with its record costs 137-151k ticks and 0.48-0.54 MB; the seon.agent/commit! after it (Datahike's commit-pending! of that one eval's datoms) 440-449k ticks and 3.19-3.24 MB, the first after the fork 7.06M ticks and 33.3 MB (seon.profile/measure!, three rounds))."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commit-allocates-what-it-writes seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run seon.db-test/a-stored-value-is-serialized-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-attribute-has-a-real-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The write holds each value to its attribute's schema (`seon.db/invalid-datom`, docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-datom), so a schema of [:map] or :any checks nothing. Checked 2026-11-06 (seon.contract/forms): :seon.model-call/error, :seon.agent/error and :seon.task/error are :seon.error/base; there is no registered key :seon.process/test-error; ``:seon.task/cause``'s :api and :evidence are :map; :seon.merge/base, ours and theirs are :seon.db/plain and :seon.merge/key is [:and {:seon.db/index true} :seon.db/plain]; :seon.db/tx-data is [:sequential :any]. Give each of those that is loose its real one; [:maybe X] under {:optional true} (absence said twice) is cleaned where met. :seon.db/tx-data tightens to ops or maps. For an attribute derived as :db.type/data the validator is [:and declared :seon.db/plain]. Then see whether `seon.contract/plainly` still has a job (seon.repl calls it, lines 639-658), and delete it if not. Measure the record-write cost proofs before and after."
