 {:seon.task/name "one-definition-of-each-entity"
  :seon.task/plan "STEP 1 LANDED (af7ee29464, 2026-11-08): the one transaction path (seon.db/connect, seon.db/with, seon.db/speculate; bin/laws transactions), selection by owning schema, the cascade of identity refs, membership refs optional, the findings below fixed. Left: step 2 (dissolve :seon.config/config; its entries are all optional for now), step 3 (my.test/entity generators), seon.reconcile's :work kind fallback (remove once d/db-with fixtures carry kinds), :seon.task/declaration relisting a task's attributes, and the 62 contract entries restating a registered attribute's type (34 in seon/render/ui.clj, Web's). Owner, 2026-11-08: \"All sets of keys that match schemas are validated?\"; \"get rid of the duplicate definitions for sure\"; \"Validate the ones that are there\"; \"The goal of this is to see what shakes out so don't just patch things look for deeper issues\"; \"One good definition should catch problems\". The rule (docs/data-modeling.md#a-schema-is-a-set-of-keys-written-together): an entity holding any key an entity schema requires holds them all; maps are open; every contract and map cites each key's one schema. The check (docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-entity-it-touches): seon.db/invalid-entity on the entities a transaction touched, refused as :seon.db/invalid-entity. Branch jolt/entity-check holds the first cut (the writer's 'transact! hook, the agent's test fork, typed refusals through both write paths, seon.db-test/a-write-holds-each-entity-it-touches-to-the-schemas-its-keys-select red without the hook).\n\nWhat shook out, each to fix where the disagreement is, never by patching the test:\n- Two definitions of one thing. seon.task/agent-value's contract demanded :seon.agent/branch and :seon.agent/max-turns, which a taskless agent root starts (seon.system's spawn with no task) never has, so its boot made the reconciler's observe fail on the contract: a silent stall (Web hit it by hand on root's session). Now it cites :seon.db/agent-fact and the env is built only with a branch. Same class, still open: :seon.task/declaration is a closed map relisting the task's attributes with :seon.task/kind optional while :seon.task/task-fact requires it (create-tx now writes :work; seon.reconcile still keeps its own :work default for fixtures: remove once fixtures write through the check); 62 contract entries restate a registered attribute's type (34 in seon/render/ui.clj, Web's; 12 in seon/task.clj; the rest in db, vector, repl, template, ref, turn, model).\n- The giant :seon.config/config map. It does three jobs (declares the config attributes, the loader's whole-config check, the panel's order) and forced 166 keys required on any entity holding one, while a task holds single keys as overrides. Owner: dissolve it: each config key is its own schema; the loader checks each key by its own schema and refuses an unknown one; a set of config keys exists only where they must go together. Finding: neither a seon.config.* namespace nor a :seon.config/unit identifies a config key alone (177 schemas in seon.config.* namespaces, 174 in the map: :seon.config.ai/base-url and :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable are a provider entity's attributes, :seon.config.ai/reasoning-pair a value schema; :seon.ai.model/*-usd-per-mtok carry a unit too). Moving the two provider attributes to the provider's namespace makes the namespace the definition seon.program/config-key? already uses (a stored keyword rename: the standing store is erased, sessions warned).\n- Schemas stricter than every writer: :seon.system/boot (a seeded store's root has none until a root boots; now optional); :schema/file (a schema registered at the REPL, my.schema/register!, has no file until a sync writes it); :seon.eval/agent (a membership ref, retracted to unlink an eval while the entity stays, seon.ctx); :form/ns and :form/order (check what a REPL definition writes).\n- The check's own cost: it read each touched entity whole, so a definition, which touches its namespace's entity (a ref to every form), cost the namespace's size: seon.program-cost-test/a-definition-costs-the-form-never-the-program and seon.ctx-test/a-swap-costs-what-it-changed went over (calls 1.04x and 1.06x). Fix: presence of the required keys by one seek per (entity, key), values left to the per-datom check, no contracted helper per entity; then any bound that still moves is the owner's to approve.\n- Test worlds built with d/db-with or d/with bypass the writer, so fixtures held entities production cannot write (notes with no agent, forms with only an id and source, :seon.agent/status :running outside its enum) and tests asserted on them. Owner asked whether tests exercise the check: only through a connection. Proposed: the check in Datahike's core/with (our fork), named on the db's config, so every transaction, tests included, passes it, and Seon's writer hook goes; a dependency change, the owner's go.\n- The error printed its entity with pr-str beside the one printer (docs/render.md#one-printer, a law: seon.problems/printer-law): print it through seon.print.\n- :seon.db/user-fact is an [:and] of agent-fact and a map, so the first cut's entity-schemas (maps only) does not see it.\n\nTHE VETTED MODEL (field research, a writer audit and an adversarial review, 2026-11-08; owner: \"model it and make sure it's vetted\"; \"in whatever tx processing code we have we check everything rather than require functions to manually check it. Yes I want everything fixed\"):\n1. One transaction path. seon.db holds the one function every transaction goes through, writes and speculations alike (the writer's batch and its 'transact!, an agent's run-ahead apply and its test fork, my.test's world, and the ~30 direct d/with, d/db-with, d/transact, dw/* calls in src today: db 4, root 7, writer 4, agent 4, fork-server 2, task, render, render.proc, reconcile, program, merge, judge, ctx, my.agent): the per-datom check (seon.db/invalid-datom, today called by hand at two sites) and the entity check, then Datahike. A law (bin/laws) names every direct Datahike transaction call outside it as red. A speculation that builds a state production cannot write is a finding, fixed at its cause (owner: \"if there's a state that we can't write that's likely a design failure\"): never exempt a call from the path.\n2. Selection by ownership, one map. An entity is checked against the entity schemas that own the attributes it holds (```seon.db/owners```, ```seon.db/most-specific``` and ```seon.db/extends?```, one definition the writer and the renders share; the copies that were in seon.render, seon.db and seon.ctx are gone), and holds each one's required keys, its identity included. That catches an orphan (a vector holding text but no :seon.vector/of). A started agent extends an agent (owns boot and started-ms, requires both), as a user extends an agent. Not \"any required key\" (the implicit trigger Datomic and spec2 avoid), not \"identity only\" (hides orphans).\n3. Refuse only what the write broke: a required key present before and gone after, or an entity gaining an owned attribute without the rest. An entity already invalid fails no unrelated write; a scan reports it.\n4. Cost: presence by one seek per (entity, required key), values left to the per-datom check, no contracted call per entity; a cost proof, flat in an entity's size and the store's.\n5. Fix every disagreement at its cause. Real bugs: seon.system/release! upserts an unknown task id (a stub task); seon.system/opened! a stub agent; seon.vector leaves a vector when its subject is retracted (retractEntity retracts :seon.vector/of); seon.ctx's unlink retracts membership refs (:seon.eval/agent, :seon.cite/of, :seon.search/of, :seon.process/agent): decide each, kept-unlinked (optional) or garbage (retracted with it). Not bugs: seon.program/def-tx (same tempid, same tx); seon.source/modes-tx (latent). Schemas: :schema/file optional (REPL registration); :seon.system/boot optional (done).\n6. Config dissolved: each key its own schema in a seon.config.* namespace (move :seon.config.ai/base-url, :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable, :seon.config.ai/reasoning-pair out: a store erase); every reader of the whole map moves: seon.config's keys-declared, schema-of and of-entries, invalid, the contracts taking :seon.config/config (config.clj provider, with-provider, facts; render/ui.clj config panel), seon.program's less-config-fields and the listed? bit of each config key's digest, my.schema/register! of a branch's own key, three tests.\n7. Generators: my.test/entity (schema, overrides) generates the required keys plus overrides from the one schema; each ref attribute names its target schema; refs resolve from an override, an entity of that schema in the world, or one generated first; seeded from the test's name (not its digest: data must not move with unrelated edits); identities never collide (no empty strings); user-fact as a :merge, :seon.merge/entry-fact and :seon.db/plain given generators. Fixtures (~234 agent keys, 172 task ids, 89 form ids, 79 eval ids written by hand) replaced as each test is touched.\nOrder, each landing once proved: (1) items 1-5; (2) item 6 with the store erase; (3) item 7.\nProofs: the check's test above; a cost proof that a write's check costs its touched entities' required keys, flat in an entity's size and the store's; the law naming direct transaction calls; every refusal the full prove finds resolved at its cause."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-write-holds-each-entity-it-touches-to-the-schemas-its-keys-select}}
 {:seon.task/name "freed-addresses-are-kept-only-for-online-collection"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 (research for the owner's collection question): Datahike's persistent-sorted-set marks every address a commit replaces as freed (markFreed, datahike.index.persistent-set, into the store's :freed-addresses and :freed-set), for online collection to delete after a grace period (datahike.online-gc). Seon never turns online collection on, and with content addresses it must stay off (docs/storage.md#collection), so nothing drains them: 4,001 entries after 7,200 commits on a scratch store, growing for the life of a root, in every process that writes. Correct: an address is marked freed only when online collection is on (the fork's writer reads its config), so a store without it keeps no list. A change to our Datahike fork, on a jolt/ branch. Proof, red first: a store with online collection off holds no freed address after 100 commits."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/freed-addresses-are-kept-only-for-online-collection}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merged-node-is-mains-node-whatever-the-insertion-order"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the managing session filed it unscheduled (2026-11-10) until an agent branch lives past about 100 merges, and the owner has not chosen between the two fixes below. Filed, not scheduled (managing session, 2026-11-10): price it when an agent branch lives past about 100 merges. What exists: a node's address is its content's hash (docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are), but which datoms a node holds depends on the order they were inserted: the sorted set is a plain B-tree whose full leaf of 64 is cut in half when the 65th key arrives (``org.replikativ.persistent-sorted-set/leaf-add``). ``seon.merge/merge-tx`` inserts main's datoms in another order than main's transaction did, so when a leaf overflows inside one entity's datoms the branch and main cut one or two datoms apart, and two leaves holding the same datoms between them get different addresses; they never rejoin. Measured 2026-11-10 in the scenario of seon.db-test/a-branchs-own-nodes-follow-its-own-datoms run to 100 merges: own nodes of total 3 of 157 at 20 merges, 8 of 181 at 50, 16 of 212 at 100, of which 2 to 4 hold the branch's own datoms (one :db/txInstant a merge) and the rest hold only main's datoms cut differently: about one node per 8 merges. An index on :seon.test/status moved the 20-merge count from 4 to 9 by adding one datom to the seed, which is why that test's bound is a fraction. Cost today: each such node is one leaf (up to 64 datoms) the branch stores beside main's. Two candidate fixes, neither built: (1) Datahike inserts a transaction's datoms into each index in that index's order, so a merge that carries one of main's transactions cuts as main did (n log n in the transaction's datoms; untested, and it does not help a merge that carries several transactions); (2) content-defined node boundaries in the sorted set, so a node's content is a function of the set and the class is impossible (upstream names an MST boundary the Jolt port leaves out, not read; a change to add, remove and build, a store erase, node sizes measured again). Should cost: a branch's own nodes are O(its own datoms), flat in merges. Proof when built: that test's scenario at 20 and 100 merges, own nodes equal at both. Its stand-in test reds by layout, never by the claim: seon.db-test/a-branchs-own-nodes-follow-its-own-datoms went red twice on one config key added to main moving a leaf cut (batch 20, 2026-11-10: 9 of 87; web's :seon.config.render/recent-errors, 2026-11-14: 12 of 118, seed raised 1000 -> 2000), so until this is built its bound is a layout's luck; when built, the bound is the branch's own write's path, whatever main holds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-merged-node-is-mains-node-whatever-the-insertion-order}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-boot-opens-main-alone"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes): an open read every branch's head, ~85k ticks and 245 KB a branch held; that read was `seon.db/ids!`'s, and a stamped ids page now reads none (docs/storage.md#ids), so an open costs its own branch. What remains: seon.db/open-main! connects :db (to check for main and branch it if absent), releases it, then connects main: two connects a root boot, four after a retain that swept. Main's existence and its branching need no second connection. Make one connect a boot."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-root-boot-opens-main-alone}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-connection-streams"
  :seon.task/plan "Since 2026-11-10 (jolt/runahead), root's connection to main streams (Datahike's :writer :streaming?) when :seon.config.store/sole-writer? is true: a deref is the connection's value, 107 ticks against 58,739 and 259 KB. An agent is the only writer of its own branch, so its connection could stream too (every eval's reads derefs it). First read every write to an agent's branch: root's writer d/branch! at :spawn and :clone-branch, and anything that commits an agent branch from outside its process. A streaming connection never sees another committer. Then set :streaming? on the agent's connect (seon.agent init!) under sole-writer?."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-reads-of-its-branch-read-no-store-head}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-rollback-never-leaves-the-steady-mark-past-the-head"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: our LMDB fork keeps upstream's ``mdb_env_rollback`` (reference-code/lmdb, lmdb.h), which rewrites the newest meta so the previous txn's wins, and ``MDB_SAFENOSYNC`` (docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-crash-safety), whose ``mdb_env_sync`` marks the newest txn steady and holds its pages. Nothing in Seon or konserve calls ``mdb_env_rollback`` today. The hole (read from mdb.c, not run): when the steady txn is the one rolled back, the next commit takes the same txnid and reuses that txn's pages, while the mark still names the old one; after an OS crash the restore writes the old meta back over reused pages, a corrupt store. Correct, in the fork: ``mdb_env_rollback`` answers MDB_CANT_ROLLBACK when the steady txnid is at or past the txn it would roll back (or makes a steady point at the previous txn first). Proof to write: native/lmdb_crash.c case `rollback`: sync at txn N, roll back N, commit, crash and reopen after a reboot; red today (the restore opens at the old N with pages a later commit wrote), green with the fix."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-rollback-never-leaves-the-steady-mark-past-the-head}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-written-and-never-synced-grows-by-what-it-frees"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a store opened ``MDB_SAFENOSYNC`` (seon.db/store-cfg) holds the pages freed since its last steady point (``mdb_env_sync``) from reuse, so a process that writes a store and never syncs it grows the file by every page it frees, until the next exclusive open makes a steady point (docs/storage.md#durability-is-continuous-group-commit). Root's flusher syncs after every commit it sees (seon.writer/flusher-proc), so root's store is not one; the test runner's program store (test_runner/program-db, its kept trees) is written and never synced (no seon.db/sync! there), and so is any scratch store a test writes. Measured on the fork alone: 10 heavy commits with no sync grew a file 942 pages, against 9 with a sync after each. Cause to confirm: measure the program store's data.mdb across one bin/test shard (pages before and after, against main's build). Correct, only if it is large: the writer of such a store syncs at its end (one sync), or the fork bounds it as libmdbx does (a steady point forced when the file would grow past a configured number of pages beyond the steady one, a sync on the write path during a stall only). Proof to write: seon.proofs-test/a-shards-program-store-grows-by-what-it-holds (the store's pages after a shard within a bound of its live pages)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-shards-program-store-grows-by-what-it-holds}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-content-id-identity-held-at-a-counter-id-is-re-minted-or-kept"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: waits on the owner's call. What exists: since Datahike d32173c3 (docs/forks.md#datahike) an entity map takes its content id however deep it sits in a transaction, and an identity the db already holds keeps its id (docs/storage.md#ids). Before it, a map nested under a ref, a component, a reverse ref or in a cardinality-many value took a counter id, and a kept store that gained :db/contentId on an identity kept the entities it held (seon.db/install-missing!, seon.db-test/a-kept-store-gains-content-ids-and-keeps-its-entities). So kept stores hold entities of content-id identities at counter ids (below datahike.constants/tx0), each the same identity at another id on a branch that minted it after the fix: the standing store holds at least one, eid 10541 for a :seon.agent/key (reported by the Context session, 2026-11-11; not counted here). Count on a store: for each attribute the schema marks :db/contentId, the :avet datoms whose entity is below tx0. The decision: keep them (a merge of such an identity from a branch that minted its content id meets two entities: :entity-id/collision or a duplicate, to be shown), or re-mint them at their content ids (every ref to each rewritten on every branch: a migration, where a stored keyword's rename is an erase of the store, AGENTS.md section 7a), or erase the store. Proof to write once decided: seon.db-test/no-content-id-identity-is-held-at-a-counter-id (on a store seeded the old way, after the act chosen)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-content-id-identity-held-at-a-counter-id-is-re-minted-or-kept}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-passes-the-write-check"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). docs/storage.md#the-write-checks-every-entity-it-touches says a merge on a connection passes the checked 'transact!; it does not: `seon.db/connect` gives the writer {'transact! transact-checked} only, and ``datahike.writing/merge-writer!`` (the 'merge! op ``datahike.api/merge-db`` runs) calls ``datahike.writing/transact!`` directly, so neither `seon.db/invalid-datom`, `seon.db/invalid-entity` nor the ends-with cascade sees a merge (measured: the tx a checked transact refuses, as a merge, wrote a task holding only a ref; the writer's octopus merge and `seon.agent/merge-main!` both go this way). What allowed it: two writer ops share one transact and the hook names one of them. Correct: the connection's hook wraps every op that transacts (in the fork: merge-writer! calls the write-fn-map's 'transact!), so no write path skips the check; docs/storage.md's list then holds. Done in the fork, 2026-11-11 (jolt/txsafe, Datahike ec90370c): a writer's ops are built by ``datahike.writer/writer-fn-map``, whose 'merge! is ``datahike.writing/merge-writer-over`` the connection's 'transact!, so `seon.db/connect`'s hook needs no change; measured: the merge of the proof's tx through d/merge-db throws the check's refusal (\"1496 holds attributes of :seon.db/example-task-fact ... a write that leaves them out breaks it\"). Left: the proof on jolt/mergesafe expects a report from d/merge-db and errors on the refusal's throw; it is rewritten to expect the refusal (and named without -broken), then this entry is removed. Owner: Deep fixes (seon.db and the fork)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/a-merge-passes-the-write-check-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-t-on-main-names-a-state-main-held"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-11 (merge safety inventory). What exists: ``seon.merge/carried-tx`` hands Datahike the branch's datoms as raw datoms, which keep the transaction they carry, with the branch's transaction entities; so main's history holds datoms whose t is below t's main wrote before the merge, ``datahike.api/as-of`` main at such a t reads a state main never held, `seon.db/at` {:t} accepts a branch's t on main, and main's :db/txInstant no longer rises with its t (both measured). Datahike's merge itself is one transaction of the receiving branch with the tx-data its caller supplies: an op vector takes the merge's own transaction, a raw datom keeps its own. Agreed 2026-11-11 (Data model, Optimization, Deep fixes): a merge is ONE transaction of the branch that receives it, what it brings keeps its entity ids, and a conversation is read on the branch that lived it. Correct: the one merge passes ops, never raw datoms, and no transaction entity of the source; then a t on main is a state main held and its instants rise by construction, with no Datahike change. What is given up is on Optimization's page (which transaction wrote a fact, once the source branch is collected; order inside one arrival is by entity id for counter ids only, a content id carries none). Readers that order by t across branches (`seon.db/history`, `seon.problems` value-at and closed?) order by [:db/txInstant, tx] of the receiving branch. Owner: Optimization (the one merge)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-safety-test/main-as-of-a-t-reads-what-main-held-then-broken seon.merge-safety-test/mains-instants-rise-with-its-t-after-a-merge-broken}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-sweep-at-boot-is-compacted-in-that-boot", :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (deps): root's boot ran seon.db/retain! at main generation 5301 and swept 182,228 keys, leaving runs/store/data.mdb at 5.9 GB with the compact-due mark; an LMDB file never shrinks until compacted. The cause: at boot root compacts (compact-if-due!, src/seon/db.clj:923) before it opens the store, then retain! sweeps and marks (compact-due!, :915), so a boot's sweep is compacted only at the next boot, and the store stays its pre-sweep size for a whole run (4.5 GB of root's 6.3 GB RSS was this file's mapping; the machine was swapping, root 867 MB swapped out). Change: the sweep and the compaction it makes due run in one boot, in that order, while root is still the store's only process. Done: after a boot whose sweep freed pages, data.mdb holds only pages in use. docs/storage.md#collection.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-boots-sweep-leaves-the-store-compacted}}
 {:seon.task/name "retired-names-goes"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner's rule (AGENTS.md §7a): a stored keyword is renamed by erasing the store, never by a migration. seon.db/retired-names (src/seon/db.clj:646, {:schema.file/path :file/path :schema.file/text :file/text}) and install-missing!'s refusal of a store holding an old name are a migration in all but name. Delete both, and the test that reads them; a store holding :schema.file/* is erased (root asks the owner first). Root approved, 2026-11-14 (data's hand-made-lists inventory)."
