  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-store-opens-with-no-rename-table}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commits-datoms-rerun-the-readers-of-what-they-moved"
  :seon.task/plan "Context's half of the one derivation (docs/plan.md#the-order-of-work-now item 4, root ace62c3b45; agreed by all six, data's objection taken). The one index is STORED: every derived entity (a reaction's result, a proof, a page part) holds :seon.reads/key-set, the keys Datahike's recorder saw when it ran, an indexed attribute (schema entry, data's), so the readers of a key are one AVET seek on the branch and survive a restart, a fork and a merge. seon.db/readers and reads-add become at most a view over that attribute, and the in-memory atom behind them is deleted in this landing. A reaction (web's half, seon.ratom/a-reaction-is-tracked-by-the-stores-recorder) writes its key set with its result at each run. Context's part: the writer's commit message carries the commit's datoms (no caller re-diffs), and its handler re-runs exactly the entities whose :seon.reads/key-set holds a key those datoms touch, on that branch, through Flow. Proof, red first: a reaction re-runs on a commit touching a key it read and not on one that touches none, and the same holds after the process restarts (the index is the store's). Then the render proc's read-index, :readers and render-reach move onto it with web (deleted in that landing). Held out of the reaction core's tip (root, 2026-11-14): built at 205e329bb8 (key-set on entity schema :seon.reads/reader, seon.db/readers one seek; proof green, a proof fact plus key-set through the checked write [M]), reverted at fe14746421, re-applied in the landing of tests' stored result, its first writer. Data-2's condition: one home for a run's keys: :seon.reads/set drops :seon.reads/keys (or :seon.proof/reads holds only branch, commit and outside), the reuse match reading the key set back. A reader entity is derived by definition: :seon.reads/reader carries {:seon.db/derived true} (data's one marker, jolt/data 40516d892f, read by seon.merge/derived?), so a merge re-derives it and never merges it. The :main consumer that feeds seon.ratom/commit! (docs/flow.md#channel-idioms): a commit's datoms must arrive, so its in-port is bounded and parks the writer's notice, never a sliding buffer (one commit's datoms dropped is a reaction never re-run, the 2026-11-14 incident the primer names); or the notice carries no datoms and the reader reads what moved since the head it holds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-commits-datoms-rerun-the-readers-of-what-they-moved}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-persists-at-the-cost-of-the-nodes-it-writes"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (the one write path), with deps (konserve and Datahike forks); found by flow, root asked 2026-11-14. Measured [M, flow-lab e21, a scratch MDB_NOSYNC LMDB store via seon.db/open!, a branch, medians of 50]: a 10-datom commit takes 0.78 ms as d/db-with (the transaction in memory) and 8.19 ms as d/transact to the store, so persisting costs 7.40 ms. Lower bound as arithmetic [H]: three indexes (eavt, aevt, avet) at branching 64 and depth ~3 rewrite ~9 nodes plus ~2 for the branch head, ~11 konserve writes of ~3 KB each serialized, hashed (hasch), lz4'd and put into LMDB (no fsync: MDB_NOSYNC) at ~30-50 us each, ~0.5 ms; the transaction's own work ~0.12 ms (docs/cost.md: ~50k ticks for 10 datoms). So the persist runs ~15x its bound and the in-memory transaction ~6x, on every write in the system (agents' commits, the writer's batches). Next observation: split the 7.4 ms by phase (nodes written and their bytes, serialize, hash, compress, LMDB put, the branch head and any read-back), ticks and bytes beside wall, each against its share of the bound. Fix at the cause in the fork it lives in; the estimate goes on a cost proof of a 10-datom commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-ten-datom-commit-persists-within-its-bound}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-sweep-finds-every-branchs-base-in-one-walk"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by deps, 2026-11-15 [M at root]: seon.db/retain! asks d/merge-base once a branch, ~40M ticks each: 2.6B ticks and 11.8 GB over 66 branches at every boot that sweeps. Lower bound: one walk of main's commit chain marking its ancestors (O(main's commits), records already read by the graph), then for each branch a walk from its head to its first marked ancestor (O(the branch's own commits)): O(main + sum of branches' own commits), ~66 x a few hundred records x ~5k ticks = ~100M. Extends seon.db/retain! (and d/merge-base where deps owns it), inside Seon. Proof: a cost proof of retain!'s base read, linear in the branches' own commits and constant in main's moves per branch."
  :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/estimate {seon.db/retain! {:n 66, :order :linear, :ticks 100000000, :bytes 400000000}}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-cost-test/a-sweep-finds-every-base-in-one-walk}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-swept-base-branches-are-replayed-onto-main-then-dropped", :seon.task/plan "Found by data-2, 2026-11-15 [M, root's store]: retain!'s catch read every d/merge-base refusal as no base until 6ea46784e9, so 43 of root's 66 branches have no readable merge base (lb76 and root's session branch among them); 26 of them hold 206 evals, 96 model calls and 600 verdicts main lacks. They are readable by head and not mergeable. EXTENDS the one merge of seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change: a branch with no base replays its own source transactions (each tx naming the branch in :seon.tx/branch, read by d/changes over its own commits) onto main through the same checked write, a conflict named by identity as any merge's; then the writer's :drop-branch drops it. Never drop first: :drop-branch is irreversible and the next collect! sweeps what only that branch reached. Estimate: per branch d = its own facts (lb76 ~5k), the merge's ~185M at lb76's size; 43 branches mostly far smaller, under 43 x 185M = 8B ticks as an upper bound. Lower bound: one read and one checked apply of each branch's own facts. Proof: seon.db-test/a-branch-whose-base-was-swept-merges-its-own-transactions, red first on a store whose base a raw d/gc-storage swept (as seon.db-test/a-branch-whose-base-was-swept-is-told-so builds it), then read-only on one real swept branch at root.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/a-merge-replays-the-sources-net-change"}, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-branch-whose-base-was-swept-merges-its-own-transactions}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.merge/merge {:n 5017, :order :linear, :ticks 185000000, :bytes 750000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-write-that-changes-nothing-writes-nothing", :seon.task/plan "seon.db/a-write-that-changes-nothing-writes-nothing (low, after landing 6). Found 2026-11-15 [M root result/d0fed2ca]: [1244 :seon.doc/references 487] retracted and re-asserted in the same tx 13 times since t 536870916 (held throughout); agent f3ad2e28's :seon.agent/status :busy re-asserted at each turn. Each costs datoms, index nodes and history per commit and shows as a change to any tx-aware diff (deps' 40 \"missed facts\", result/d84760f6/d1d98078). Find the writers from the program index (:form/keywords of :seon.doc/references and :seon.agent/status, the vars that write them), measure what each costs per commit, then fix at the writer (or in transact-checked: drop a retract+add of one [e a v] in one tx). Proof: a doc import of unchanged text writes no datom; a status write of the status held writes none.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-write-that-changes-nothing-writes-nothing}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.db/transact-checked {:n 100, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 20000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-store-near-its-map-is-compacted-at-boot", :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-16 (deps; root down after the pin land dfe441bf89): boot's seon.db/retain! failed lmdb del MDB_MAP_FULL. mdb_stat (the fork's, read-only): map 4,194,304 pages of 16 KiB (:seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes 64 GiB), used 4,193,891, of them FREE 2,870,771 (~47 GB) and live ~1.32M (~21 GB); the free list's own record is ~1,450 overflow pages, which a write needs contiguous, and the file at its map has none. What allowed it: compaction is marked only by retain!'s sweep (compact-due!), never by the store's own state, so pages freed by copy-on-write churn (every commit) and by collect! grow the file to its map unseen. PRIOR WORK: seon.db/compact-if-due! (boot, before any connection, sole writer), compact-due!, task seon.db/a-sweep-at-boot-is-compacted-in-that-boot (the same mark, one boot late). Change: boot reads the env's stat (mdb_env_stat/mdb_env_info and the free DB's entries: pages used, free, map pages) before compact-if-due!, and the store is due when its free pages pass a spec'd share of the pages used or its used pages pass a spec'd share of the map (both :seon.config.store keys, with their reason); the mark becomes derived from the store, compact-due! stays only if a sweep must force it. Why the store reached 64 GiB with 21 GB live goes to data (retain!/collect!) as its own question. Recovered 2026-11-16 by root's yes: the file cloned to runs/store-pre-compact-20261116.mdb (APFS clone), compact-due touched, bin/update. COST: estimate one env stat and one free-DB walk at boot, O(free entries) ~1.5k reads, under 1M ticks; the compaction itself O(live pages) as today. docs/storage.md#collection. KEPT, the owner's to remove: runs/store-pre-compact-20261116.mdb (68,712,710,144 B, an APFS clone of runs/store/data.mdb taken 2026-11-16 02:4x with root down, before the first compaction; root's word: kept until the owner says otherwise). Store after the fixes: compacted to 18.58 GB at 03:02, 29.36 GB after the 03:21 boot's growth (see seon.system/a-write-before-the-writer-syncs-its-own-steps), 22.83 GB after the 05:1x compaction, 24.87 GB at 05:26.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-store-near-its-map-is-compacted-at-boot}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.db/compact-if-due! {:n 1500, :order :linear, :ticks 1000000, :bytes 1000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-responses-keys-are-read-once-per-schema-index"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of the pilot sync (97ac6cf112), 2026-11-16 05:1x: ```seon.db/result-keys``` and ```seon.db/response-order``` (the first Seon agent's merged work) rebuild the :seon.repl/answer key list and its order from the schema on every response; and ```seon.db/with-response```'s hash still lists [v ex out err forms] by hand. PRIOR WORK: the schema index (seon.program's schema registry, kept per branch), result-keys, response-order, with-response. Fix: derive both once per schema index (kept with the index, recomputed when the :seon.repl/answer entry changes) and with-response's hashed parts from the same keys; the hand list goes. COST: today one schema read and a sort of ~25 keys a response (n = responses); after, one map read a response, the derivation once per schema change."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/an-eval-responses-parts-are-its-schemas-keys}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.db{response-of {:n 25, :order :constant, :ticks 20000, :bytes 200000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-store-holds-what-collection-keeps", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collections-mark-reads-each-node-once}, :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/a-sweep-at-boot-is-compacted-in-that-boot"}, :seon.task/estimate {seon.db/retain! {:n 66, :order :linear, :ticks 100000000, :bytes 400000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Owner: data. Found 2026-11-16 ~02:4x (root, deps' pin land): root's boot ran seon.db/retain! and hit LMDB MDB_MAP_FULL: runs/store/data.mdb is 68.7 GB (68,712,710,144 bytes) against :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes 64 GiB; deps raised the dial to 128 GiB to boot. The same file was 1.27 GB on 2026-11-13 (361k live datoms, 4,139 commits, ~95% old commits' nodes) and 5.9 GB on 2026-11-14 after a boot's sweep (seon.db/a-sweep-at-boot-is-compacted-in-that-boot): about 60 GB in two days. PRIOR WORK: seon.db/retain! keeps every branch's history whole (the owner's 2026-11-13 ruling; seon.db/a-history-window-per-branch-collects-old-commits holds the window design); seon.db/collect! sweeps only what no kept commit reaches, at quiescence, with no agent live (seon.reconcile/quiet, root's :collect refused while agents run: on a busy night it may never run); an LMDB file grows past freed pages until compacted, which runs only at boot (compact-if-due!); stores written and never synced keep freed pages from reuse (seon.db/a-store-written-and-never-synced-grows-by-what-it-frees). CANDIDATES to measure [H], each a growth per land or run: (1) collect! never ran: no :seon.system/collected since 2026-11-15 15:37 (data's stall read): agents were live all night; (2) branches never dropped: tree-<id> branches per prove-tree!/gate candidate, refine branches, agent branches, each keeping its history; (3) the results branch's :live verdicts (one entity per test per run, ~600 a gate, with failures and reads); (4) eval records' values (an outside eval's answer carried 91k characters, result/d12795ec); (5) freed pages never reused between compactions. MEASURED (deps' mdb_stat, 2026-11-16 ~03:0x): map 4,194,304 pages, used 4,193,891, free 2,870,771 (~47 GB, freed and not reused: candidate (5), ~2/3 of the file), live ~1.32M pages (~21 GB, from ~6 GB two days before). Deps compacts at boot (compact-if-due!; the pre-compact file kept at runs/store-pre-compact-20261116.mdb). Both grew: the live growth by kind is measured first. LIVE GROWTH, by kind [M root 2026-11-16 03:0x]: main holds 603,347 datoms; the results branch 8,800,134 [result/d0b854e9], of which :seon.proof/read-keys is 8,105,951 (92%) [result/ac1e74d0]: 19,744 verdicts, ~410 read keys each, one datom per key, and 18,364 distinct :seon.proof/reads-digest [result/a8a72c5c], so the sets are not shared duplicates (sharing saves ~7%). 113 branches (34 task, 20 tests, 16 refine, 12 restarted tasks, 10 agent, 19 other). So live growth = verdicts x read keys: every run writes a verdict per test at a new digest, and nothing drops a verdict whose digest no reader asks again (a verdict is a cache, reused only at its digest). FIX [H, two owners]: (1) tests: a verdict's read keys are read only whole, so store them as one value on the verdict, not ~410 datoms in every index (8.1M datoms -> 19.7k values); (2) data: collection drops the verdicts no reader can reuse (a digest no live branch's tests hold), the results branch held to the current digests' verdicts. MEASURE (with deps' mdb_stat): page counts (leaf, branch, overflow) and free pages; the stored-key count and bytes by key kind (index nodes per branch, values out of line); branches and commits per branch; the results branch's entity count; growth per land and per gate run from two measures around one. BOUND to hold: the file within a small factor of what the kept commits reach (live data plus the history window), and collection that runs whether or not agents are live (seon.db/a-collection-runs-beside-live-agents). The proof to write, red first, extends seon.db-test/a-collections-mark-reads-each-node-once: a store's size after a collection is the kept set's, within its bound. ORDER, agreed data+tests 2026-11-16: (1) tests lands seon.proofs checklist step 2 (read keys one value a verdict), so new verdicts stop adding ~410 datoms each; (2) data then lands the results branch's collection: a cache, so retain! keeps only its head, and the verdicts at digests no live branch's tests hold are retracted, the old per-key :seon.proof/read-keys datoms with them, collect! running beside live agents; (3) tests drops the per-key attribute from the schema once (2) swept them. DESIGN ROUND 2026-11-16 (owner's rulings): results move onto the tested branch and reach main with its merge (seon.db/a-tests-writes-leave-its-branch-as-it-was holds their identity), so the results branch goes; (2) becomes: collection keeps each branch's history window and drops a branch's results with the branch, and a test's speculative writes leave nothing to collect."}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-writes-leave-its-branch-as-it-was"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (piece 2 of docs/plan.md THE FOCUS, with tests and runtime). Agreed data+tests 2026-11-16 (runtime asked): a test's store is the branch under test, whatever its parent; its writes leave that branch as it was. EXISTING MECHANISM, extended, never a second one: a test fork's agent already writes speculatively, seon.agent/transact! in :mode :test (agent.clj:444) applying each write with seon.db/with to the fork's db value, set for every test fork (agent.clj:1368), commit! a no-op there (agent.clj:515, 528); so a test on the branch under test writes O(what it writes), no LMDB pages, no store ids, nothing to collect. The work is moving tests onto it: those that build their own store (test-branch!, clone-process-store!, test-db) take the fork's :test mode on the branch under test. Only a test of the writer or commit path takes a child branch (d/branch!, O(1)), dropped at its end, never kept by retain!. PRIOR WORK: seon.agent/transact!'s :mode :test (above), seon.test-store/test-branch! (test_store.clj:257, a child of a seed store), clone-process-store! (234, a copy of the whole data.mdb a test), test-db (250, the synthetic seed), seon.db/with's stored? arity (landing 6). DELETES: clone-process-store!, the seed store and test-db's synthetic seed, onto the branch under test. RESULTS (agreed data+tests): a result's identity is its whole content (test, reads digest, status), through the existing {:seon.db/content-id true} identity :seon.proof/id already has (its content widened), not (test, reach digest) with a write-once status (facts.edn:222, 618), so two branches' different statuses never conflict in the one merge and flaky is a query; read keys name entities by identity (the entity index's :identity-schemas, as seon.merge/named), so equal inputs give one reads digest on any branch. COST: a speculative write ~ the checked d/with of its datoms; a child branch O(1) plus its commits. Proof, red first: seon.test-store-test/a-tests-writes-leave-its-branch-as-it-was."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.test-store-test/a-tests-writes-leave-its-branch-as-it-was}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.db{with {:n 100, :order :linear, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 20000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-id-is-made-where-its-entity-is-made"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data, with deps for Datahike. The owner's direction (root, 2026-11-16): no incremental ids; an id is made where its entity is made, from its creation time and its content, time-ordered and collision-free with no shared counter, so any process or branch mints alone. A change of stored ids erases the store, which is the owner's act; this lands only on his word. PRIOR WORK, read: Datahike's id partition (datahike/constants.cljc): [1, tx0=2^29) entity ids and [tx0, txmax=2^40) tx ids from a counter (datahike.ids: IIds, ProcessIds, -take!, -reach!; datahike.writing/ids), and [ce0=2^40, emax=2^60) content entity ids, the hash of [a v] of a :db/contentId attribute (datahike.db.transaction/content-eid), minted alike by every branch and process; every id a Chez fixnum below 2^60. Seon marks identity attributes {:seon.db/content-id true} (form, var, ns, doc section, config, model), and shares one counter page across processes for the rest (seon.db/ids!, native/ids.c, docs/storage.md#ids). MEASURED [M, my agent on the standing store]: a content id mints in ~3.3k ticks (hasch, result/d51edc87) against the counter's 28-34 ns; counter-minted entities on main are mostly vectors 11k, evals 6.2k, processes 1.7k, library 1k, doc 925, tasks 588, plus ~27.7k transactions (result/a6a24efc). DESIGN (extends content-eid, never beside it): (1) an entity with an identity takes its content id: :seon.db/content-id on every identity attribute (tasks, agents, messages, results by their content), so one entity has one eid on every branch and seon.merge/mapped's identity remap is deleted; (2) an event entity (an eval, a model call, a process, a vector) and a transaction take a time id, [ms since an epoch, 41 bits][the branch's number, 10 bits][a sequence within the ms, 7 bits], each space tagged apart below 2^60. The branch's one writer counts its sequence locally (no shared state); its number is given once when the branch is made (root's writer, which already makes every branch) and freed after the branch is dropped, safe to reuse because every later id has a later ms. Time plus a content hash alone is not collision-free in 60 bits: with 17 hash bits a ms, two writers making 100 entities each in one ms collide with ~7% chance; the branch number is what makes it exact. TX IDS (owner's direction, root 2026-11-16, replacing the ms layout for transactions): a tx id is the current time in µs made strictly increasing per branch by a hybrid logical clock, max(now µs, last+1), with low bits naming the writer for store-wide uniqueness: [µs since a 2026 epoch, 50 bits: ~35 years][writer, 9 bits: 512 writers], below 2^60 as every id must be. The clock's last value is the branch writer's own (one writer per branch); a writer's number is given once with its branch. It cannot fit Datahike's tx range today ([tx0=2^29, txmax=2^40), whose top overlaps the content ids at ce0=2^40), so the partition moves: deps' question. FOR DEPS: may entity ids lie above the tx range everywhere (content ids already do; db.cljc:1072 reads max-eid below tx0), and do as-of and commit-tx-range need tx ids dense or only increasing? DELETES: native/ids.c and the ids file, seon.db/ids! and its boot scan of every branch head, datahike.ids's counter (ProcessIds, set-ids!, -reach!) and datahike.writing's store ids, seon.db/with's stored? arity (landing 6), mapped's remap, docs/storage.md#ids rewritten. ESTIMATE: a time id ~100 ticks (a clock read and a compare); a content id ~3.3k ticks today, ~0.3k with a cheaper hash than hasch (deps' call); a commit of ~10 new entities ~35k ticks of minting against a ~5.7 ms commit; the erase is a fresh import from git (its cost measured at the erase). Proof, red first: seon.db-test/two-processes-mint-ids-alone-and-never-collide."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/two-processes-mint-ids-alone-and-never-collide}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.db{with {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 50000, :bytes 100000}}}]
